export default "## What a Week After Your Period Actually Means\n\nA week after your period ends is not a random point in your cycle. It often falls close to ovulation. For someone with a 28-day cycle and a period that lasts five days, day 12 to 14 frequently involves a hormonal shift that can produce a small amount of spotting. If your cycle runs shorter or longer, the timing shifts accordingly.\n\nUnderstanding where you are in your cycle is the first step in interpreting any mid-cycle bleeding. A tracker that records cycle start dates over several months can help you estimate where your approximate ovulation window actually falls.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only. It is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## The Most Common Causes\n\n### Ovulation Spotting\n\nWhen the dominant follicle ruptures to release an egg, a brief drop in estrogen can cause the uterine lining to shed a small amount of blood. This typically appears as pink or light brown discharge. It lasts one to two days at most and coincides with other signs of ovulation. These include stretchy, clear cervical mucus and sometimes mild one-sided pelvic cramping, called mittelschmerz.\n\nOvulation spotting is considered a normal variation. Many people never experience it. Others see it consistently. Some notice it only occasionally.\n\n### Hormonal Fluctuation\n\nEven without a clear ovulatory event, fluctuations in estrogen and progesterone across the cycle can occasionally cause light spotting. This is more common in people whose cycles are already somewhat irregular or who are in their late 30s or early 40s when hormonal patterns begin to shift.\n\n### Implantation Bleeding\n\nIf conception occurred during the current cycle, light spotting around days 6 to 12 after ovulation may represent implantation. Implantation bleeding occurs about six to twelve days after fertilization, not six to twelve days after your last period. The overlap with ovulation spotting in terms of appearance, light, pink or brown, and brief, makes these easy to confuse.\n\n### Post-Sex Spotting\n\nThe cervix has a rich blood supply and can bleed with friction. This is particularly likely if cervical ectropion is present. Spotting that consistently follows intercourse and has no other pattern is most likely cervical in origin. It is usually benign but worth mentioning at a routine gynecological appointment.\n\n### Polyps and Fibroids\n\nUterine polyps and fibroids can cause irregular bleeding at any point in the cycle. Bleeding from these sources tends to be more variable in timing and often heavier than ovulation spotting. If the same spotting pattern recurs across multiple cycles without a clear mid-cycle correlation, a pelvic ultrasound can evaluate for structural causes.\n\n## What the Blood Looks Like Matters\n\nThe appearance of mid-cycle spotting gives useful information:\n\n- **Pink or light brown**: Often older blood mixed with discharge. Common with ovulation spotting and implantation bleeding.\n- **Bright red**: Fresher blood. More associated with cervical sources or structural causes.\n- **Heavy flow or clots**: Not consistent with spotting. If bleeding approaches period-level flow outside of your period, contact a healthcare provider.\n\nLogging color, texture, and estimated volume, such as a spot on underwear versus requiring a pad, gives you data you can actually use.\n\n## How to Track Mid-Cycle Spotting\n\nTracking a single episode of spotting tells you very little. Tracking it over three to six cycles tells you whether it is a consistent part of your pattern or something new.\n\nFor each episode, log:\n\n- **Cycle day**, count from day 1 of your last period\n- **Duration**, how many hours or days it lasted\n- **Appearance**, color and volume estimate\n- **Any accompanying symptoms**, pelvic pain, discharge, pain during sex\n- **Relevant context**, recent intercourse, stress, medication changes, illness\n\nOver a few cycles, consistent mid-cycle spotting at the same approximate cycle day is strongly suggestive of ovulation. Spotting that has no pattern, comes with other symptoms, or is increasing in volume is worth discussing with a provider.\n\n## The Privacy Dimension\n\nMid-cycle spotting is the kind of specific, detailed symptom data that reveals a great deal about your reproductive health. Apps that store this data on cloud servers may share it with third parties for advertising purposes or produce it under legal compulsion.\n\nFlo Health settled an FTC enforcement action in 2021 after sharing users' period and symptom data with Facebook and Google. In states where abortion is criminalized, period tracking data, including records of irregular bleeding or missed cycles, is not covered by HIPAA and can be subpoenaed. An on-device tracker stores your logs only on your phone, where they cannot be accessed by the company that made the app.\n\n## When to See a Healthcare Provider\n\nMost mid-cycle spotting is benign, but see a provider if:\n\n- Spotting is heavier than a few drops or lasts more than two to three days\n- It is accompanied by pelvic pain, fever, or abnormal discharge\n- It occurs consistently after sex\n- It is a new pattern in someone who previously had very regular cycles\n- Spotting occurs after menopause, which always warrants evaluation\n\nA single episode of light spotting at mid-cycle in an otherwise regular, asymptomatic person typically does not require urgent attention. A pattern that is changing, worsening, or accompanied by other symptoms does.";
